WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.792
Safe logging in C++ begins with purpose.

2
00:00:02.792 --> 00:00:07.179
Log events should support debugging, operations, monitoring, response, or audit needs.

3
00:00:07.179 --> 00:00:13.960
If an event has no clear purpose, it is harder to justify the cost and data risk.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.157
Minimize sensitive data.

5
00:00:15.157 --> 00:00:22.336
Avoid secrets, tokens, session identifiers, raw request bodies, full object dumps, unnecessary personal data, and excessive implementation details.

6
00:00:22.336 --> 00:00:28.319
Redact before data reaches the sink, and treat logs as a possible sensitive data store.

7
00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:31.909
Prefer structured events with stable names and safe fields.

8
00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:38.290
Use fixed templates, keep untrusted values separate, encode or escape control characters, and bound long values.

9
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.279
Log output should preserve record integrity for humans and tools.

10
00:00:42.279 --> 00:00:44.273
Handle diagnostics safely during failure.

11
00:00:44.273 --> 00:00:53.048
Separate user-facing errors from internal logs, keep logging safe during cleanup, and avoid hiding the original failure behind a secondary logging

12
00:00:53.048 --> 00:00:53.447
problem.

13
00:00:53.447 --> 00:00:55.840
Reliability matters as much as content.

14
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.632
Finally, govern logs after they are written.

15
00:00:58.632 --> 00:01:05.811
Protect access, define retention, separate environments, review redaction policy, and treat logging changes as security-sensitive engineering work.

16
00:01:05.811 --> 00:01:09.800
Logs are both a safety tool and a data responsibility.

17
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.794
The lasting habit is intentionality.

18
00:01:11.794 --> 00:01:22.165
Before adding a log line, decide who needs it, what decision it supports, what data it contains, how it behaves under load, and how long the

19
00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:23.760
resulting record should live.
